A stack is the right choice for an undo/redo system because it follows the Last In, First Out (LIFO) principle. When a user performs several actions, the most recent action is usually the first one they want to undo. For example, if a user types text, deletes text, and then changes the formatting, the formatting change should be undone first. A stack naturally supports this behavior through its push and pop operations. Using two stacks also makes it possible to manage both undo and redo operations. One stack stores previous states, while the other stores states that can be restored.

A queue is better suited for a help desk because it follows the First In, First Out (FIFO) principle. Customers should generally be served in the order their requests were received. When a customer submits a ticket, it is added to the rear of the queue. When an employee is ready to help someone, the ticket at the front is removed. This creates a fair and predictable order for processing customer requests.

My implementations differ from Python's built-in lists because they use individually allocated Node objects connected through pointers. The stack uses a top pointer, while the queue uses front and rear pointers. No Python list operations are used to store the stack or queue elements. This provides practice with the underlying concepts that data structures such as stacks and queues are built upon. It also makes the behavior of operations such as push, pop, enqueue, and dequeue explicit rather than relying on Python's built-in data structures.
